NEUTRINO MIXING  —  flavor vs mass eigenstates

FLAVOR STATES                         MASS STATES
(weak interaction basis)              (propagation basis)
detected in charged-current           travel as definite-mass
weak processes                        wave packets

  νe  (electron neutrino)  ──┐
                             │     ┌──  ν1   (mass eigenstate 1)
  νμ  (muon neutrino)      ──┼─────┤
                             │     ├──  ν2   (mass eigenstate 2)
  ντ  (tau neutrino)       ──┘     └──  ν3   (mass eigenstate 3)

Fundamentals
  • Flavor states |να⟩  (α = e, μ, τ)  are what W± produce and detect
  • Mass states   |νi⟩  (i = 1, 2, 3)  are the Hamiltonian eigenstates that propagate
  • They are related by the PMNS mixing matrix U:

        |να⟩  =  Σi  Uαi  |νi⟩

        |νi⟩  =  Σα  U*αi |να⟩

  • Because the |νi⟩ have different masses, they pick up different phases
    e^{-i E_i t} as they travel. Superposition therefore changes with L/E
    → neutrino oscillation (flavor conversion).

  • |Uαi|^{2} = probability that a flavor-α neutrino is found in mass state i
    (and vice versa). Mixing angles θ12, θ23, θ13 plus CP phase δ_CP
    parametrize U.

Key point: a neutrino is created as one flavor, but immediately becomes
a coherent mixture of three masses. After distance L it is a different
mixture of flavors.
